## 2. ⚠️ Bigger finding in the az-014 allow-list

Reading the allow-list, these are **not** on it:

| Not allowed | What it breaks |
|---|---|
| `Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachineScaleSets` (only permitted inside AKS-managed resource groups) | The VMSS autoscaling design, plus the VMSS-based agent pool I proposed |
| `Microsoft.Compute/galleries` | Gallery, which you already saw denied |
| `Microsoft.Compute/images` | The managed-image fallback I offered earlier, so scrap that |

This is from reading the rule text. It isn't confirmed until a VMSS create is attempted, which needs the VNet first. **Allowed** and useful: `Microsoft.Web/*` (App Service), `Microsoft.Network/*` (App Gateway, private endpoints), `Microsoft.DBforPostgreSQL/flexibleServers`, `Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines`, `disks`, `snapshots`, `Microsoft.ContainerService/*` (AKS), `Microsoft.App/containerApps`, and `Microsoft.DevOpsInfrastructure/*` (Managed DevOps Pools, which replace the VMSS agent pool).

So the GCP golden-image + MIG pattern can't be reproduced without two policy exemptions. That is an architecture decision for you.

| Option | Cost | Complexity | Downtime | Policy |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| A. App Service (Linux, Premium v3, autoscale) | Per-instance plan cost, no VM patching | Medium: Apache, PM2 and Packer work is dropped, and the app must listen on App Service's port | None (slots) | Types allowed, no exemption |
| B. VMSS + Gallery via exemption | Lowest | Low (the design already built) | None | ⚠️ Two exemptions, timeline unknown |
| C. Fixed VMs, no autoscale | VM cost × N | Medium: no golden image, pipeline deploys to the VMs | Manual rolling | Allowed |
| D. AKS | Highest | High | None | Allowed |

**Recommendation: A.** It is the only option that uses already-allowed types, it has built-in autoscale and zone redundancy (verify the SKU requirements), and it removes the OS-patching CPU spikes you debugged on GCP. Trade-offs: the Packer, Apache and VMSS work so far goes unused, the dev team must confirm the apps run on an App Service Node runtime, and private access still needs the VNet and private endpoints from the SR. You can add a B exemption request to the SR at no cost, but don't wait on it.

